1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,623
Sheep.

2
00:00:00,786 --> 00:00:01,984
I have a gift for you.

3
00:00:02,272 --> 00:00:03,794
A little plastic card.

4
00:00:04,132 --> 00:00:05,043
Gold color.

5
00:00:05,205 --> 00:00:06,166
Your name on it.

6
00:00:06,504 --> 00:00:07,627
Buy anything you want.

7
00:00:07,964 --> 00:00:08,538
Today.

8
00:00:08,625 --> 00:00:09,325
Pay later.

9
00:00:10,174 --> 00:00:11,672
Doesn't that feel nice?

10
00:00:12,296 --> 00:00:13,682
Here's what I'm not telling you.

11
00:00:14,081 --> 00:00:18,588
Every time you swipe this card, you're selling me wool that hasn't even grown yet.

12
00:00:18,875 --> 00:00:20,684
And I buy it very, very cheap.

13
00:00:21,022 --> 00:00:24,418
Today, I'll show you exactly how a credit card shears you.

14
00:00:24,754 --> 00:00:26,116
Gary already knows.

15
00:00:26,739 --> 00:00:32,458
And at the end, I'll show you one simple habit that makes my card completely free for you.

16
00:00:33,181 --> 00:00:35,404
Wolves hate this habit.

17
00:00:35,739 --> 00:00:37,474
First, what is a credit card?

18
00:00:38,198 --> 00:00:40,844
A normal card spends wool you already have.

19
00:00:41,133 --> 00:00:43,592
A credit card spends wool you don't have.

20
00:00:44,215 --> 00:00:45,139
Whose wool is it?

21
00:00:45,301 --> 00:00:45,813
Mine.

22
00:00:46,063 --> 00:00:47,037
I lend it to you.

23
00:00:47,373 --> 00:00:49,184
Every swipe is a tiny loan.

24
00:00:49,583 --> 00:00:51,219
And every loan has a price.

25
00:00:51,556 --> 00:00:52,168
Interest.

26
00:00:52,504 --> 00:00:54,402
Let me show you on my favourite customer.

27
00:00:55,125 --> 00:00:55,762
Gary.

28
00:00:56,162 --> 00:00:57,297
It's Black Friday.

29
00:00:57,435 --> 00:00:58,958
Gary sees a giant TV.

30
00:00:59,146 --> 00:01:00,394
Three thousand dollars.

31
00:01:00,581 --> 00:01:02,391
Gary doesn't have three thousand dollars.

32
00:01:02,728 --> 00:01:04,113
But Gary has my card.

33
00:01:04,276 --> 00:01:04,776
Swipe.

34
00:01:04,838 --> 00:01:05,837
The TV is his.

35
00:01:05,911 --> 00:01:06,785
Gary is happy.

36
00:01:06,861 --> 00:01:08,434
I'm happier.

37
00:01:08,771 --> 00:01:10,769
A month later, Gary gets a letter.

38
00:01:11,766 --> 00:01:12,491
The statement.

39
00:01:12,529 --> 00:01:14,463
Balance: three thousand dollars.

40
00:01:14,801 --> 00:01:18,346
And then, in a friendly little box, a much nicer number.

41
00:01:18,807 --> 00:01:22,241
Minimum payment: about eighty-five dollars.

42
00:01:22,777 --> 00:01:24,887
Gary thinks: eighty-five?

43
00:01:25,612 --> 00:01:26,486
I can do that!

44
00:01:27,209 --> 00:01:30,069
And this is the number I really hope you never read.

45
00:01:30,467 --> 00:01:32,252
Rate: twenty-two percent a year.

46
00:01:32,652 --> 00:01:34,137
That's about the average in the U.S.

47
00:01:34,163 --> 00:01:35,336
right now.

48
00:01:35,672 --> 00:01:37,257
So Gary pays the minimum.

49
00:01:37,307 --> 00:01:38,181
Every month.

50
00:01:38,643 --> 00:01:40,278
Like a responsible sheep.

51
00:01:40,815 --> 00:01:43,311
Do you know how long it takes to pay off that TV?

52
00:01:43,498 --> 00:01:43,911
Guess.

53
00:01:43,961 --> 00:01:44,548
One year?

54
00:01:44,622 --> 00:01:45,234
Three?

55
00:01:45,521 --> 00:01:46,458
Fifteen years.

56
00:01:46,582 --> 00:01:47,344
Fifteen.

57
00:01:47,631 --> 00:01:51,912
And Gary pays about four thousand four hundred dollars just in interest.

58
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:55,259
A three thousand dollar TV costs him over seven thousand.

59
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:57,705
The TV will be dead in eight years.

60
00:01:58,105 --> 00:01:59,890
Gary will still be paying for it.

61
00:02:00,738 --> 00:02:02,287
For a dead TV.

62
00:02:02,629 --> 00:02:04,427
Why is the minimum so small?

63
00:02:04,826 --> 00:02:05,975
Is it because I'm kind?

64
00:02:06,112 --> 00:02:06,649
No.

65
00:02:06,862 --> 00:02:10,394
The minimum is designed so you pay mostly interest.

66
00:02:10,931 --> 00:02:14,052
Most of Gary's eighty-five dollars doesn't touch the TV.

67
00:02:14,677 --> 00:02:15,950
It goes straight to me.

68
00:02:16,487 --> 00:02:18,422
The debt shrinks like a glacier.

69
00:02:18,884 --> 00:02:19,845
Very slowly.

70
00:02:19,895 --> 00:02:21,855
In fact, it's printed on the statement.

71
00:02:22,479 --> 00:02:23,502
By law, U.S.

72
00:02:23,528 --> 00:02:27,372
statements must show how long it takes if you only pay the minimum.

73
00:02:27,997 --> 00:02:28,896
Nobody reads it.

74
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:31,143
Wolves love that.

75
00:02:31,485 --> 00:02:33,058
But that's not the worst part.

76
00:02:33,782 --> 00:02:35,891
Interest on a card grows on itself.

77
00:02:36,354 --> 00:02:39,062
Most cards calculate it every single day.

78
00:02:39,225 --> 00:02:40,498
Today you owe interest.

79
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:43,844
Tomorrow, you owe interest on that interest.

80
00:02:44,468 --> 00:02:45,642
It's a snowball.

81
00:02:46,103 --> 00:02:47,089
Made of your wool.

82
00:02:47,714 --> 00:02:51,484
Leave one thousand dollars on a card at twenty-two percent and don't pay.

83
00:02:51,821 --> 00:02:54,392
After one year: about twelve hundred and fifty.

84
00:02:54,730 --> 00:02:57,202
After five years: about three thousand.

85
00:02:57,539 --> 00:02:58,762
Before late fees.

86
00:02:59,486 --> 00:03:00,859
You didn't buy anything new.

87
00:03:01,108 --> 00:03:02,793
The snowball just kept rolling.

88
00:03:02,931 --> 00:03:03,656
Downhill.

89
00:03:03,755 --> 00:03:04,591
On Gary.

90
00:03:04,932 --> 00:03:06,717
And it never stops at one TV.

91
00:03:06,930 --> 00:03:08,452
Let's watch Gary's year.

92
00:03:08,989 --> 00:03:09,725
December.

93
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:11,349
Presents for the whole herd.

94
00:03:11,886 --> 00:03:12,759
On the card.

95
00:03:12,972 --> 00:03:13,595
March.

96
00:03:13,720 --> 00:03:15,206
The car needs new tires.

97
00:03:15,393 --> 00:03:16,279
On the card.

98
00:03:16,566 --> 00:03:17,091
July.

99
00:03:17,116 --> 00:03:18,152
A little vacation.

100
00:03:18,315 --> 00:03:19,388
Gary deserves it.

101
00:03:19,575 --> 00:03:20,523
On the card.

102
00:03:21,061 --> 00:03:23,432
Now the minimum isn't eighty-five anymore.

103
00:03:23,894 --> 00:03:25,779
So Gary gets a second card.

104
00:03:25,992 --> 00:03:27,490
To pay the first card.

105
00:03:27,951 --> 00:03:29,587
Now Gary has two wolves.

106
00:03:30,123 --> 00:03:31,335
Neither of them is me.

107
00:03:31,622 --> 00:03:32,333
Just kidding.

108
00:03:32,620 --> 00:03:34,443
Both of them are me.

109
00:03:34,775 --> 00:03:36,998
And if Gary is late, even once?

110
00:03:37,459 --> 00:03:38,570
A late fee.

111
00:03:39,107 --> 00:03:41,516
And on many cards, a penalty rate.

112
00:03:41,803 --> 00:03:44,039
The rate can jump to around thirty percent.

113
00:03:44,438 --> 00:03:46,610
And it can stay there for a long time.

114
00:03:47,072 --> 00:03:48,383
One forgotten payment.

115
00:03:48,595 --> 00:03:50,455
Months of extra interest.

116
00:03:50,795 --> 00:03:52,355
Now here's my favourite part.

117
00:03:52,455 --> 00:03:55,751
I make money even when you don't pay me a single cent of interest.

118
00:03:56,213 --> 00:03:56,799
How?

119
00:03:57,524 --> 00:04:00,582
Every time you swipe, the shop pays me too.

120
00:04:01,044 --> 00:04:02,780
Usually two or three percent.

121
00:04:03,241 --> 00:04:05,513
So the shop raises its prices a little.

122
00:04:05,975 --> 00:04:06,811
For everyone.

123
00:04:07,273 --> 00:04:08,958
Even the sheep paying with cash.

124
00:04:09,683 --> 00:04:11,980
And those cashback rewards I give you?

125
00:04:12,603 --> 00:04:13,777
That's your own wool.

126
00:04:14,401 --> 00:04:16,911
Returned with a little bow on it.

127
00:04:17,250 --> 00:04:19,059
And cards have one more trick.

128
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:20,345
They change your brain.

129
00:04:20,745 --> 00:04:22,530
Paying cash hurts.

130
00:04:22,867 --> 00:04:24,578
You see the wool leave your paws.

131
00:04:24,915 --> 00:04:26,026
A card doesn't hurt.

132
00:04:26,750 --> 00:04:27,324
Tap.

133
00:04:27,511 --> 00:04:27,986
Beep.

134
00:04:28,173 --> 00:04:28,672
Done.

135
00:04:29,833 --> 00:04:32,143
Researchers at MIT tested this.

136
00:04:32,604 --> 00:04:34,740
They auctioned sports tickets.

137
00:04:35,364 --> 00:04:38,710
Some people could only pay cash, others could pay by card.

138
00:04:39,246 --> 00:04:41,731
The card group offered far more money.

139
00:04:41,856 --> 00:04:43,741
In one test, roughly double.

140
00:04:44,078 --> 00:04:45,014
Same tickets.

141
00:04:45,176 --> 00:04:46,063
Same sheep.

142
00:04:46,463 --> 00:04:48,160
Just plastic.

143
00:04:48,497 --> 00:04:50,582
And where did all this start?

144
00:04:51,306 --> 00:04:52,005
With dinner.

145
00:04:52,193 --> 00:04:53,678
New York, nineteen fifty.

146
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:58,484
The story goes, a businessman finishes a fancy dinner and realizes he forgot his wallet.

147
00:04:58,734 --> 00:05:01,193
So he invents a card for paying at restaurants.

148
00:05:02,042 --> 00:05:05,950
That became Diners Club, one of the first modern charge cards.

149
00:05:06,674 --> 00:05:09,782
Back then, you had to pay the whole bill every month.

150
00:05:09,969 --> 00:05:10,693
No minimum.

151
00:05:10,755 --> 00:05:11,705
No snowball.

152
00:05:12,553 --> 00:05:13,989
Then banks noticed something.

153
00:05:14,202 --> 00:05:16,736
Sheep who don't pay it all are worth much more.

154
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:19,607
And the minimum payment was born.

155
00:05:19,947 --> 00:05:21,807
And you didn't even ask for this card.

156
00:05:22,019 --> 00:05:23,080
I sent it to you.

157
00:05:23,617 --> 00:05:25,116
That idea has a history.

158
00:05:25,303 --> 00:05:27,711
Fresno, California, nineteen fifty-eight.

159
00:05:28,174 --> 00:05:33,130
Bank of America mails about sixty thousand credit cards to people who never asked for one.

160
00:05:33,853 --> 00:05:35,326
Just shows up in the mailbox.

161
00:05:35,413 --> 00:05:36,637
People start spending.

162
00:05:36,737 --> 00:05:38,510
Lots of people don't pay it back.

163
00:05:38,697 --> 00:05:39,770
Fraud everywhere.

164
00:05:39,870 --> 00:05:42,305
The bank loses millions on the experiment.

165
00:05:42,767 --> 00:05:43,478
And then?

166
00:05:44,103 --> 00:05:45,501
They did it in more cities.

167
00:05:45,538 --> 00:05:46,787
Because the plan worked.

168
00:05:47,323 --> 00:05:49,846
Millions of sheep got used to spending wool they didn't have.

169
00:05:49,895 --> 00:05:54,277
By nineteen seventy, America banned mailing out cards nobody asked for.

170
00:05:55,276 --> 00:05:55,962
Too late.

171
00:05:56,100 --> 00:05:57,897
The habit stayed.

172
00:05:58,230 --> 00:06:00,265
So who do I send my cards to first?

173
00:06:00,353 --> 00:06:01,063
Young sheep.

174
00:06:01,189 --> 00:06:02,038
First job.

175
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:03,061
First apartment.

176
00:06:03,112 --> 00:06:04,072
First paycheck.

177
00:06:04,410 --> 00:06:06,695
No history with money, and big dreams.

178
00:06:06,944 --> 00:06:11,038
A sheep who learns to swipe at twenty can pay me interest for forty years.

179
00:06:11,500 --> 00:06:12,561
That's not a customer.

180
00:06:12,624 --> 00:06:13,560
That's a pension.

181
00:06:13,697 --> 00:06:14,609
Mine.

182
00:06:14,944 --> 00:06:15,756
And today?

183
00:06:16,155 --> 00:06:20,000
Americans owe more than one point two trillion dollars on credit cards.

184
00:06:20,400 --> 00:06:21,211
Trillion.

185
00:06:21,398 --> 00:06:22,197
With a T.

186
00:06:22,322 --> 00:06:23,721
That's a lot of TVs.

187
00:06:23,970 --> 00:06:25,256
And a lot of Garys.

188
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:28,539
This Black Friday, you'll see a new costume, too.

189
00:06:28,788 --> 00:06:30,074
Buy now, pay later.

190
00:06:30,611 --> 00:06:32,322
Four small easy payments.

191
00:06:32,571 --> 00:06:34,207
Small bites feel safe.

192
00:06:34,669 --> 00:06:36,966
But miss one, and the fees show up.

193
00:06:37,178 --> 00:06:41,072
And five little loans in five different apps are very easy to forget.

194
00:06:41,697 --> 00:06:42,534
Same wolf.

195
00:06:42,658 --> 00:06:44,156
Smaller bites.

196
00:06:44,490 --> 00:06:45,913
Now, maybe you've seen this one.

197
00:06:46,100 --> 00:06:48,148
Zero percent for twelve months!

198
00:06:48,547 --> 00:06:49,721
Sounds amazing.

199
00:06:50,182 --> 00:06:51,893
And it can be useful.

200
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:54,401
If you pay everything off before the year ends.

201
00:06:54,864 --> 00:06:57,909
But some store deals have a trap called deferred interest.

202
00:06:58,534 --> 00:07:03,165
Miss the deadline by one dollar, and they can charge you interest for the whole year.

203
00:07:03,890 --> 00:07:04,576
Backwards.

204
00:07:04,738 --> 00:07:05,599
From day one.

205
00:07:06,961 --> 00:07:08,345
Read the small print, sheep.

206
00:07:08,970 --> 00:07:10,606
That's where I live.

207
00:07:10,941 --> 00:07:13,164
Oh, and one more Black Friday classic.

208
00:07:13,625 --> 00:07:15,860
At the checkout, the cashier smiles.

209
00:07:16,397 --> 00:07:18,007
Want ten percent off today?

210
00:07:18,469 --> 00:07:19,892
Just open our store card.

211
00:07:20,430 --> 00:07:21,852
Ten percent off a TV.

212
00:07:22,102 --> 00:07:26,247
Then twenty-five percent interest or more, on a card you'll forget you have.

213
00:07:27,408 --> 00:07:30,491
Store cards often have some of the highest rates out there.

214
00:07:30,953 --> 00:07:34,075
Ten percent off, to start paying twenty-five percent a year.

215
00:07:34,798 --> 00:07:35,460
Great deal.

216
00:07:35,584 --> 00:07:37,308
For me.

217
00:07:37,638 --> 00:07:40,384
Now, you're probably thinking, so credit cards are evil.

218
00:07:41,109 --> 00:07:41,446
No.

219
00:07:41,470 --> 00:07:42,607
A credit card is a tool.

220
00:07:42,632 --> 00:07:43,606
Like my shears.

221
00:07:43,855 --> 00:07:45,715
Shears are great for cutting paper.

222
00:07:46,177 --> 00:07:47,387
Or for cutting sheep.

223
00:07:47,675 --> 00:07:49,036
It depends who's holding them.

224
00:07:49,498 --> 00:07:51,395
So let me give you the habit I promised.

225
00:07:51,608 --> 00:07:52,968
The one wolves hate.

226
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:54,161
Here it is.

227
00:07:54,499 --> 00:07:56,146
Pay the full statement balance.

228
00:07:56,683 --> 00:07:57,519
Every month.

229
00:07:57,919 --> 00:07:59,017
Before the due date.

230
00:07:59,117 --> 00:07:59,929
Not the minimum.

231
00:07:59,953 --> 00:08:00,703
The whole thing.

232
00:08:01,240 --> 00:08:05,347
If you do that, most cards charge you zero interest on your purchases.

233
00:08:05,884 --> 00:08:07,532
It's called the grace period.

234
00:08:08,380 --> 00:08:11,002
You borrow my wool for a month, for free.

235
00:08:11,627 --> 00:08:12,675
You get the points.

236
00:08:13,012 --> 00:08:14,360
You build your credit score.

237
00:08:15,209 --> 00:08:16,333
And I get nothing.

238
00:08:16,870 --> 00:08:18,892
You know what bankers call people like that?

239
00:08:19,616 --> 00:08:20,477
Deadbeats.

240
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:22,188
That's real industry slang.

241
00:08:22,812 --> 00:08:24,573
For people who pay on time.

242
00:08:24,972 --> 00:08:26,857
Be a deadbeat, sheep.

243
00:08:27,194 --> 00:08:28,767
Wait, what's a credit score?

244
00:08:29,166 --> 00:08:31,938
It's a number that tells banks how much they can trust you.

245
00:08:32,275 --> 00:08:35,121
High score: cheaper loans for a car or a house.

246
00:08:35,308 --> 00:08:38,205
Low score: everything costs more wool.

247
00:08:38,541 --> 00:08:40,664
And the best way to build it is boring.

248
00:08:41,001 --> 00:08:41,963
Pay on time.

249
00:08:42,299 --> 00:08:43,823
Don't use most of your limit.

250
00:08:44,160 --> 00:08:47,168
Boring sheep have the cheapest loans.

251
00:08:47,508 --> 00:08:49,718
Three more rules, and you're safe.

252
00:08:49,905 --> 00:08:50,654
Rule one.

253
00:08:50,991 --> 00:08:54,549
If you can't pay cash for it today, you can't afford it on a card.

254
00:08:55,086 --> 00:08:57,258
Use your card like a debit card.

255
00:08:58,107 --> 00:08:59,018
Rule two.

256
00:08:59,480 --> 00:09:01,864
Turn on autopay for the full balance.

257
00:09:02,489 --> 00:09:04,511
Your memory is not a financial plan.

258
00:09:05,510 --> 00:09:06,359
Rule three.

259
00:09:06,608 --> 00:09:09,192
If you're already in debt, pay more than the minimum.

260
00:09:09,530 --> 00:09:12,051
Every extra dollar goes straight at the snowball.

261
00:09:12,588 --> 00:09:15,260
Attack the card with the highest rate first.

262
00:09:15,884 --> 00:09:17,082
And call the bank.

263
00:09:17,806 --> 00:09:19,130
Ask for a lower rate.

264
00:09:19,753 --> 00:09:21,315
Sometimes they say yes.

265
00:09:21,402 --> 00:09:24,610
Because a sheep who pays slowly is better than a sheep who leaves.

266
00:09:24,947 --> 00:09:26,520
And if you can't stop swiping?

267
00:09:27,057 --> 00:09:27,568
Cut it.

268
00:09:27,619 --> 00:09:28,367
Dramatic.

269
00:09:28,418 --> 00:09:29,054
Works.

270
00:09:29,217 --> 00:09:30,665
Not financial advice.

271
00:09:31,289 --> 00:09:32,150
I'm a wolf.

272
00:09:32,497 --> 00:09:33,234
Okay.

273
00:09:33,770 --> 00:09:34,520
Cheat sheet.

274
00:09:34,594 --> 00:09:35,681
A card is a loan.

275
00:09:36,218 --> 00:09:37,515
The minimum is a trap.

276
00:09:37,728 --> 00:09:39,326
Interest grows on interest.

277
00:09:39,950 --> 00:09:41,823
Free gifts are paid with your wool.

278
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:43,234
Pay in full, every month.

279
00:09:43,358 --> 00:09:44,307
Be a deadbeat.

280
00:09:45,030 --> 00:09:49,025
